  "evidence": "A: Freeze non-essential immigration with secure detention/offshore processing and rapid deportations; recruit 40,000 police, expand stop and search, tougher sentencing incl. automatic life for violent repeat offenders, 10,000 more prison places; raise defence spending to 3% GDP and create a Veterans Department; target zero NHS waiting lists with patient vouchers and 20% tax relief on private healthcare, charge for no-shows; tighten welfare with a two-strike rule; cut foreign aid by 50%; prioritize locals in social housing.\nB: Cut NHS waiting times via evening/weekend appointments and shared lists; National Care Service; 8,500 extra mental health staff; dentistry rescue; public health measures (smoke-free generation, youth vape limits, junk food ad bans); Neighbourhood Policing Guarantee, tougher antisocial behaviour/shoplifting response, specialist rape units and fast-track rape courts, mandatory police vetting; new Border Security Command with more caseworkers and enforcement; NATO and nuclear commitments with path to 2.5% GDP on defence and veteran support; New Deal for Working People (day-one rights, ban exploitative zero-hours and fire-and-rehire, minimum wage remit), major social/affordable housebuilding; abolish non-dom and close carried interest loophole.",
  "reasoning": "Given a profile that prioritises Care/Harm first, then Authority and Loyalty, with notable weight on Equality, B places stronger emphasis on reducing harm through the NHS, social care, mental health, dentistry and public health, while also reinforcing policing standards, border enforcement and national defence, and advancing equality via workers’ rights, social housing and tax fairness. A aligns strongly with Authority and Loyalty through policing, immigration crackdowns and higher defence, and offers some Care via NHS access, but its emphasis on welfare tightening and lower egalitarian focus makes it a weaker fit to the Care- and Equality-weighted profile.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "B"
}
